## 1 Introduction
Long-afterglow molecular systems, such as ultralong roomtemperature phosphorescence (RTP) materials, have been booming as growing demands for the development in many areas, such as displays [1–3], bio-imaging [4–6], optical amplifiers [7], information encryption [8–12], and detection [13,14]. Generally, according to Kasha’s rule, excitons are original from the lowest excited state, and the photoemission is highly stable regardless of the excitation wavelength [15]. This usually leads to the single-color phosphorescence of the materials, and thus the multi-color triplet-emitters are still scarce [16]. Dynamic color-tunable photoemission, especially time-dependent ultralong phosphorescence, requires relatively complex transitions, such as energy transfer between multiple triplet levels and inter-system crossing (ISC) between singlet and triplet states [17,18]. However, energy transfer may cause the annihilation of intermediate-state luminescent species, leading to the disappearance of simultaneous multiple-band RTP, and therefore materials with time-resolved RTP are relatively limited [19]. To meet the need of promising time-gated imaging technology [20], the design of dynamic long-afterglow materials, particularly with time-resolved color-tunable photoemission and photonic information transformation ability, remains highly desirable.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as porous coordination polymers, have attracted much attention in the fields of drug delivery [21], catalysis [22], identification sensing [23], gas adsorption [24], and luminescence [25]. During last few years, long-afterglow MOFs have been discovered and developed rapidly based on the facile design principles [26–29]: (1) selection of organic ligands with abundant carbonyl and/or heteroatoms facilitates the promotion of both ISC and spin-orbit coupling (SOC); (2) periodic ordering of the MOFs could enhance structural rigidity and reduce the non-radiative transition of the chromophore; (3) suitable metal ions (such as $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ) are beneficial to enhancing the SOC by heavy atomic effect. However, these basic rules are not sufficient to solve the problem for multicolor ultralong phosphorescence. Diverse photoemission pathways (including metal-to-ligand charge transfer (MLCT), ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT), and/or ligand-to-ligand charge transfer (LLCT)) need to be rationally designed and leveraged. By virtue of pore and/or cage structures, the incorporation of guest molecules into the host MOFs can not only affect the host-guest structures through different intermolecular interactions, but also modify the energy transfer path and the luminescence accordingly [30]. However, understanding the effect of host-guest interactions on dynamic long-afterglow properties is still rather limited.
Herein, we put forward that replacing small guest molecules within host frameworks could alternate the structural symmetry of the MOFs matrix, in which regular and distorted structures could reduce and increase the energy transfer pathway respectively. This strategy may serve as an effective way to construct multi-color phosphorescent emission (Scheme 1). In this work, we have firstly designed a new class of Cd-MOFs by the use of 4,5-imidazole dicarboxylic acid (IMDC) as the ligand. The multiple coordination sites of IMDC led to the formation of $\upmu_{5}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{L}$ and $\upmu_{4}$ - L units as the “wavy” planes and columns of the frameworks (Figure 1), respectively. The different metal-ligand self-assembly fashions are beneficial to achieving multiple energy levels. Computational and experimental results showed that the incorporation of $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ dimethylformamide (DMF) into MOF (Cd-DMF) exhibited a dual-phosphorescence emission, which occurred through a combination of LLCT (ca. $560\mathrm{nm}$ , $\tau=187.0\mathrm{ms}$ and ligand self-emission (ca. $530\mathrm{nm}$ , $\tau=196.0\mathrm{ms}$ ) due to structural distortion. Taking advantage of the wide range of absorption in the visible region, CdDMF was used as a host to accommodate dye molecules, and tunable cold-warm white-light emitting can be further obtained via phosphorescence resonance energy transfer (PRET) [31,32]. Therefore, this work not only constructs new types of multi-color ultralong phosphorescence based on the host-guest MOFs, but also develops the guest-induced structural changes for tailoring the energy transfer routes.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials and reagents
All the reagents (4,5-imidazoledicarboxylic acid, cadmium nitrate tetrahydrate, Eosin Y disodium salt and Rhodamine B) were purchased from Sigma Chemistry Co. Ltd. (USA) and used without further purification.
## 2.2 Synthesis of metal-organic frameworks
The crystalline Cd-DMF, EY2-10@Cd-DMF, RhB@CdDMF, and $\mathrm{Cd-CH_{3}C N}$ samples were prepared via the solvothermal method. Typically, a mixture of $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{(0.2~mmol}$ , 0.0616 g) with 4,5-imidazoledicarboxylic acid $(0.2\mathrm{mmol}$ , $0.0312{\mathrm{~g}})$ , $6~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $4\mathrm{mL}$ DMF was stirred for $10\mathrm{min}$ . The mixture was then transferred to a Teflon reactor $(23~\mathrm{mL})$ and heated at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $72\mathrm{{h}}$ . Then, the mixture was cooled at a rate of $5\ \mathrm{{^\circCh}^{-1}}$ to room temperature. The yellow transparent crystals were washed by ethanol $\mathrm{10~mL)}$ for three times. EY2-10 $@$ Cd-DMF was prepared via the similar method but by further adding Eosin Y disodium salt $(0.0010\ \mathrm{g})$ . RhB $@$ Cd-DMF was prepared via the similar method but by further adding Rhodamine B $(0.0010\ \mathrm{g})$ . $\mathrm{Cd-CH_{3}C N}$ sample was prepared by the similar method with Cd-DMF but replacing DMF by $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ .
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Construction of MOFs
Through a solvothermal method, we synthesized two structurally similar three-dimensioanl (3D) MOFs (namely Cd
Cd-DMF was densified and stable, which was confirmed by the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) results (Figure S4), with the structure maintaining up to $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Compared with Cd-DMF, $\mathrm{Cd-CH_{3}C N}$ exhibited a more regular structure, with the molecular solvent position replaced by a $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and a free $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ molecule in the structural pore (Figure S1b). In Figure 1d, the ligands in the $\mathrm{Cd-CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ structure showed an axisymmetric arrangement, and the pore spacing $(7.60\mathrm{\AA})$ was smaller than that of Cd-DMF $(8.14\mathrm{\AA})$ . Solvent molecules in the pore channels thus modulated the pore size and affected the framework structure through intermolecular forces, with $\mathrm{C-H}^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\pi$ interactions in Cd-DMF (2.58, 2.76, 3.10, and $3.71\mathrm{\AA}$ ) stronger than that of $\mathrm{Cd-CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ (3.33, 3.09, 4.15, and $4.48\mathrm{\AA}$ ) (Figure 1e), resulting in a tilting and twisting host structure for the Cd-DMF.
## 3.2 Excitation-dependent and white-light-emitting diode (WLED) excited ultralong phosphorescence
To investigate photophysical properties of the as-obtained MOFs, luminescent spectra were tested. Upon excitation at $326\mathrm{nm}$ , C $\mathrm{\cdotd-CH_{3}C N}$ exhibited a blue-green luminescence, and faint green afterglow emission at $505\mathrm{nm}$ $\mathit{\Omega}^{\prime}\tau=88.5\mathrm{ms}$ ) after switching off the excitation light source (Figures S1 and S5). Cd- $.\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ exhibited nearly the same afterglow emission as the pristine ligand IMDC [34], suggesting that the afterglow of $\mathrm{Cd-CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ MOF mainly originated from the ligand. However, for Cd-DMF, the MOF sample exhibited an obviously different excitation-dependent luminescence and multi-color afterglow in a wide range (Figure 2a). Upon excitation from 270 to $470\mathrm{nm}$ , the luminescence of Cd
DMF was mainly concentrated at ca. 330, 450, and $560\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2b). Under delayed mode, the luminescent spectra showed a single peak, which gradually shifted from 530 to $560\mathrm{nm}$ at different excitation wavelengths $(270-470\ \mathrm{nm})$ , corresponding to the change in the afterglow emission from green to orange color (Figure 2d). By excitation with a $365\mathrm{nm\UV}$ source, the afterglow changed from yellow to green, with $530\mathrm{nm}$ (0.1 ms delayed, $\tau=187.0\mathrm{ms}$ ) gradually replacing $560\mathrm{nm}$ (360 ms delayed, $\tau=196.0\mathrm{ms}$ ) as the main emission location (Figure 2e and Figure S6). At ambient condition, the difference in the excitation spectra for the emission at 530 and $560\mathrm{nm}$ proved that the color-tunable afterglow emissions were derived from different triplet excited states (Figure S7). The multiple excited states can be reflected by ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) absorption spectra (Figure S2b), which showed that Cd-DMF had stronger absorption in the range $400{\-}650\ \mathrm{nm}$ relative to $\mathrm{Cd-CH_{3}C N}$ .
To further investigate the mechanism and the origin of the excitation-dependent luminescence and color-tunable afterglow of the Cd-DMF, variable-temperature PL spectroscopy has been carried out (Figures S8 and S9). Under $370\mathrm{nm}$ excitation, the emission intensity increased with decreasing temperature (Figure 2c and Figure S8). At $100\mathrm{K}$ (Figure S9), the luminescence intensity around ca. $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ was significantly enhanced, and the fine structures of the emission peak at ca. $330\mathrm{nm}$ could be clearly observed. The lifetimes at $454\mathrm{nm}$ ( $\dot{\tau}=391.9\mathrm{ms}$ ), $500\mathrm{nm}$ $\dot{\tau}=556.9\mathrm{ms}$ ) and $560\mathrm{nm}$ ${\mathit{\Omega}}^{\prime}{=}421.1~\mathrm{ms}$ ) were significantly different (Figure S9c), and thus can be assigned to different emissive energy levels. The luminescence at ca. $500\mathrm{nm}$ was similar to that of the pristine ligand, which inferred that this emission can originate from the triplet state of IMDC. It is noted that the emission at ca. $450\mathrm{nm}$ had a lifetime of 391.9 ms at $100\mathrm{K}$ , but only 3.42 ns at room temperature (Figures S9c and S10). The lifetime at $450\mathrm{nm}$ increased significantly with decreasing temperature and was the dominant luminescent position at both $100\mathrm{K}$ and ambient conditions. Therefore, it is deduced that the emission at ca. $450\mathrm{nm}$ could be attributed to delayed fluorescence (DF) due to triplet-triplet annihilation (TTA). The low temperature reduced the vibration and rotation of the molecules in the MOF structure, further rigidifying the structure and stabilizing the triplet exciton, causing the TTA process occurring as the concentration of triplet excitons increased. The 2D delayed spectrum (Figure 2f) revealed that the long-lived luminescence at room temperature was located in the range of $560{-}600\mathrm{nm}$ , besides those from ligandbased intrinsic phosphorescence (ca. $500{-}530~\mathrm{nm}$ ).
The photoluminescence quantum yields (PLQYs) of CdDMF and $\mathrm{Cd-CH_{3}C N}$ were $6.28\%$ and $2.48\%$ , respectively (Table S4). Inspired by the wide visible light absorption and multi-exciton generation, white-light LED excitation of phosphorescence was also explored (Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ ). Upon excitation by a commercial white-LED light source consisted of 454 and $580\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S11), a bright orange-yellow afterglow was observed for about 4 s after switching off the light source. Therefore, the introduction of multiple triplet excited states with strong UV-vis absorption may serve as an effective way to obtain LED excited long-afterglow. To the best of our knowledge, such a white-light-excited afterglow system is still very limited among state-of-the-art ultralong phosphorescence materials [35,36], particularly for the metal-organic coordination compounds.
## 3.3 Theoretical calculations
To further reveal the singlet and triplet state paths in hostguest MOFs, theoretical calculations of metal-organic coordination segment (Figure 3a) have been performed firstly. The calculation results showed that the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) mainly populated on the carboxyl groups, while the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) was delocalized on both imidazole and carboxyl groups. Therefore, the intramolecular charge transfer occured for the whole part of $\upmu_{4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{L}$ unit during the photoexcitation process. Moreover, periodic density functional theory (DFT) calculation was performed on the unit cell of MOFs (Figure 3a and Figure S12). For $\mathrm{Cd-CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}_{}$ , frontier orbitals showed obvious charge delocalization, exhibiting an LLCT from “layer” to “column” regions of MOFs. While for Cd-DMF, the locations of HOMOs and LUMOs showed a reversed LLCT process. There is nearly no electronic population on both guest molecules, indicating that different LLCT routes between $\mathrm{Cd-CH_{3}C N}$ and Cd-DMF can be mainly related to structural changes of host caused by alternated guests. Compared with the regular structure of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ , the Cd-DMF exhibited a more twisted framework, which enhanced the interactions between ligands. For example, as described above, the $\mathrm{C-H}^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\pi$ interactions within Cd-DMF were in the range of 2.58 to $3.71\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , while in Cd$\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ were 3.09 to $4.48\mathrm{\AA}$ (Figure 1e). The strong intermolecular interaction enhanced the multiple electronic coupling between ligands linked by $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ (i.e., LLCT), and thus promoted the multiple energy levels and diversed emission pathways, coresponding to the multi-color afterglow and strong visible absorption as described above.
## 3.4 Excitation-dependent cold and warm white-light emitting
The excitation-dependent luminescent spectra of EY2- $10@\mathrm{Cd}$ -DMF (Figure S16) showed that its main luminescence was at $570\mathrm{nm}$ $\tau=206.21~\mathrm{ms}$ ) (Figure S17), while the luminescence intensity at ${510}\mathrm{nm}$ was significantly lower compared with that before doping, corresponding to the emission from the single molecule rather than the aggregate, similar to the EY2 solution (Figures S2b, S18, and S19). The PLQY value increased from $0.28\%$ to $4.96\%$ (Table S7). Upon incorporation into MOFs, the long-lived photoemission of the fluorescent dye was successfully achieved by PRET between the energy donor (D) and the acceptor (A). According to the equation ${\cal E}_{\mathrm{P}}=1-\tau_{\mathrm{DA}}/\tau_{\mathrm{D}}$ , where $\tau_{\mathrm{DA}}$ ( ${\bf\dot{\boldsymbol{\tau}}}=$ $154.6\mathrm{ms}\dot{}$ ) (Figure S20) and $\tau_{\mathrm{D}}$ $\mathit{\Omega}^{\prime}\tau=196.0\mathrm{ms})$ ) (Figure S6) were the PL lifetime values of D in the presence and absence of A, respectively (DA: EY2-10 $@$ Cd-DMF; D: Cd-DMF; A: EY2), an energy transfer (ET) efficiency of $21.1\%$ could be derived [39]. Moreover, the color temperature of the whitelight emission could be adjusted by changing the excitation wavelength in a wide excitation range of $160\mathrm{nm}$ (250‒ $410\mathrm{nm})$ ) for EY2-10@Cd-DMF (Figure 4). For $\operatorname{RhB}\ @\operatorname{Cd-}$ DMF, the main long-lived luminescence corresponded to the characteristic position of the single molecule RhB $(578\mathrm{nm})$ (Figures S21 and S22), with the PLQY of $7.08\%$ (Table S7) higher than those of Cd-DMF $(6.28\%)$ and RhB $(0.18\%)$ , achieving a $1+1>2$ synergistic effect.
In view of the wide excitation range of EY2-10@Cd-DMF with tunable cold-warm white light, we further selected an LED chip $365\mathrm{nm})$ as the excitation source and made singlecomponent white light devices from both EY2-10 $@$ Cd-DMF and Cd-DMF respectively (Figure 5a, b and Figure S23). The color rendering index (Ra) stands for an important parameter to evaluate color effect of the artificial light resource. Generally, the higher the Ra, the better the reproduction of the original color of the object. Current commercial white LEDs combining blue LED chips and yellow YAG: $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ phosphors usually have an Ra less than 80, due to the lack of red light components. Also, the synthesis of inorganic components requires high temperature calcination [40]. Here, the Ra value of EY2-10@Cd-DMF and Cd-DMF was 88.4 (Figure 5b) and 80.7 (Figure 5a), respectively. Particularly, the Ra of EY2-10 $@$ Cd-DMF is higher than that of most of recently reported pure inorganics and inorganic-organic hybrid systems. Benefiting from high Ra value, easy preparation and thermal stablity (Figure S4), EY2-10@Cd-DMF is expected to be promising as white-lighting LEDs applications. In addition, in view of the long-afterglow luminescence of CdDMF, time-dependent, excitation-dependent and white-lightbased anti-counterfeiting patterns (Figure 5b) have been designed respectively to explore the application in information storage.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, beyond the color-tunable afterglow from welldeveloped pure organics (such as molecular crystals and carbon dots), this work developed an alternative route to achieve a wide range of PL tunability with excitation- and time-dependent ultralong phosphorescence by the construction of host-guest MOFs. The key to the color-tunable afterglow is to fabricate multiple metal-organic condonation sites and guest-induced distorted host framework, which facilitates the formation of multiple energy levels and LLCT states, as confirmed by both experimental and computational results. The tightly packed 3D rigid structure also stabilizes the triplet exciton and achieves effective PRET process upon incorporation of dye guests. Diverse excitation-dependent and time-dependent afterglow endows host-guest MOFs with promising applications in information encryption, which can be facilely used even by excitation with LED in daily life. Furthermore, the cold-warm white-light emitting can be switched by alternation of excitation, which is suitable for different application scenarios alternatively in day and night. To the best of our knowledge, such a dynamic afterglow tunability is much wider than that of current PL systems with excitation dependence (Table S11). It can be expected that the multiple generation of triplet excitons through host-guest energy transfer and LLCT can be extended to other hybrid systems for display, lighting and information encryption applications (Figure 5).
